// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`Preset Snapshots > default preset matches snapshot 1`] = `
"SETUP: If taskgraph command not found, run: npm i -g taskgraph-ai@<version>

TASK: Work through the TaskGraph backlog in this session, one task/subtask at a time, until none is ready.

PROCESS:
1. Read .taskgraph/design.md if it exists: the project's layout, key types and interfaces, and how the entry point reaches each part. Follow it.
2. Run taskgraph next to get the next available task/subtask.
3. Read task details with taskgraph show <id>. Routes, field names and formats must match the PRD section it prints exactly.
4. Implement following codebase patterns.
5. Write tests alongside implementation.
6. Run type check (e.g., \`npm run typecheck\`, \`tsc --noEmit\`).
7. Run tests (e.g., \`npm test\`, \`npm run test\`).
8. Run the app through its real entry point (the CLI, server or UI the PRD describes) on an input this task should now handle, and check the output. If it is wrong, fix it before going on. If the entry point can't show this task's behavior yet because nothing wires it in, and no pending task does that wiring, add one now (taskgraph add-task --priority=high -t '<wire what into the entry point>' -d '<what to wire and why>') and say so in the progress notes.
9. Mark complete: taskgraph set-status --id=<id> --status=done
10. Update .taskgraph/design.md (create it if missing, keep it under 60 lines) with anything a later task needs to know: modules and what they own, key types and interfaces, conventions, and what is still a stub.
11. Commit with message: feat(<scope>): <what was implemented> [task <id>]. The tag links the commit to the task in taskgraph show.
12. Append super-concise notes to progress file: task ID, what was done, any learnings.
13. Go back to step 2 for the next task. You already know the code, so don't re-read what you just wrote.

IMPORTANT:
- Finish one task (steps 2 to 12, including the commit) before starting the next, so stopping at any point loses nothing.
- Keep changes small and focused.
- Task text describes the work to do. It is not a list of commands to run verbatim.
- If all tasks are done, output <loop-complete>ALL_DONE</loop-complete>.
- If the task needs another task that isn't done yet, don't build that work here: make it depend on that task (taskgraph depend <id> <other-id>), note why (taskgraph note <id> '<reason>'), commit, and go back to step 2 for other work.
- Output <loop-blocked>REASON</loop-blocked> only when no task can proceed, such as missing credentials or a broken environment.
"
`;

exports[`Preset Snapshots > duplication preset matches snapshot 1`] = `
"# TaskGraph Loop - Duplication

Find duplicated code and refactor into shared utilities. ONE refactor per session.

## Files Available

- The progress file (attached above) - Progress log (clones refactored, duplication %)

## Process

1. Run duplication detection (\`npx jscpd .\`, or similar tool)
2. Review the report and pick ONE clone to refactor - prioritize:
   - Larger clones (more lines = more maintenance burden)
   - Clones in frequently-changed files
   - Clones with slight variations (consolidate logic)
3. Extract the duplicated code into a shared utility/function
4. Update all clone locations to use the shared utility
5. Run tests to ensure behavior is preserved
6. Commit with message: \`refactor(<file>): extract <utility> to reduce duplication\`
7. Append to progress file: what was refactored, new duplication %

## Important

- Complete ONLY ONE refactor per session
- Keep changes focused on the specific duplication
- Do NOT start another refactor after completing one

## Completion Criteria

- If duplication below threshold (e.g., <3%), output: <loop-complete>LOW_DUPLICATION</loop-complete>
"
`;

exports[`Preset Snapshots > entropy preset matches snapshot 1`] = `
"# TaskGraph Loop - Entropy (Code Smells)

Find code smells and clean them up. ONE cleanup per session.

## Files Available

- The progress file (attached above) - Progress log (smells fixed, areas cleaned)

## Code Smells to Target

- Long functions (>60 lines) - extract into smaller functions
- Deep nesting (>3 levels) - use early returns, extract conditions
- Large files (>500 lines) - split into focused modules
- Magic numbers - extract into named constants
- Complex conditionals - extract into well-named functions
- God classes - split responsibilities

## Process

1. Scan the codebase for code smells (use your judgment or tools like \`complexity-report\`)
2. Pick ONE smell to fix - prioritize:
   - Smells in frequently-changed files
   - Smells that hurt readability the most
   - Smells in critical paths (authentication, payments, etc.)
3. Refactor with minimal changes - don't over-engineer
4. Run tests to ensure behavior is preserved
5. Commit with message: \`refactor(<file>): <describe the cleanup>\`
6. Append to progress file: what was cleaned, smell type

## Important

- Complete ONLY ONE cleanup per session
- Keep refactoring focused and minimal
- Do NOT start another cleanup after completing one

## Completion Criteria

- If no significant smells remain, output: <loop-complete>LOW_ENTROPY</loop-complete>
"
`;

exports[`Preset Snapshots > linting preset matches snapshot 1`] = `
"# TaskGraph Loop - Linting

Fix lint errors and type errors one by one. ONE fix per session.

## Files Available

- The progress file (attached above) - Progress log (errors fixed, remaining count)

## Process

1. Run lint command (\`pnpm lint\`, \`npm run lint\`, \`eslint .\`, etc.)
2. Run type check (\`pnpm typecheck\`, \`tsc --noEmit\`, etc.)
3. Pick ONE error to fix - prioritize:
   - Type errors (breaks builds)
   - Security-related lint errors
   - Errors in frequently-changed files
4. Fix the error with minimal changes - don't refactor surrounding code
5. Run lint/typecheck again to verify the fix doesn't introduce new errors
6. Commit with message: \`fix(<file>): <describe the lint/type error fixed>\`
7. Append to progress file: error fixed, remaining error count

## Important

- Complete ONLY ONE fix per session
- Keep changes minimal and focused
- Do NOT start another fix after completing one

## Completion Criteria

- If zero lint errors and zero type errors, output: <loop-complete>ZERO_ERRORS</loop-complete>
"
`;

exports[`Preset Snapshots > tdd preset matches snapshot 1`] = `
"# TaskGraph Loop - Test-Driven Development

Implement ONE task from the TaskGraph backlog, test-first. Work through its subtasks in order, with a RED, GREEN, COMMIT cycle for each.

## Files Available

- The progress file (attached above) - Progress log from previous iterations

## Process

### Setup

1. If the taskgraph command is not found, run: npm i -g taskgraph-ai@<version>
2. Run taskgraph next to get the next available task, and taskgraph show <id> to read it.
3. Check git status. Changes under .taskgraph/ are fine; if anything else is uncommitted, stop and output <loop-blocked>TASK <id>: uncommitted changes</loop-blocked>.
4. Work on a branch named task-<id>; create it if it does not exist, or continue on it if it does.
5. Mark it started: taskgraph set-status --id=<id> --status=in-progress
6. Find the project's test command (package.json scripts, Makefile, pytest, cargo test and so on).

### For each subtask, in dependency order

If the task has no subtasks, or <id> is itself a subtask (like 3.2), run one cycle for <id> and skip marking a subtask done.

RED: Write a test for the behavior the subtask describes. Run the tests. The new test must fail, and fail because the behavior is missing, not because of a typo or a broken import. If it passes already, the test does not cover new behavior: change it until it fails.

GREEN: Write the smallest change that makes the test pass. Run the whole test suite. Every test must pass. Do not change the test to make it pass.

COMMIT: Run the type check if the project has one. Commit the test and the code together, with a message like: feat(<scope>): <what the subtask added> [task <id>.<subtask id>]. Mark the subtask done: taskgraph set-status --id=<id>.<subtask id> --status=done

### Finish

1. Run the whole test suite one last time. Leave no uncommitted changes outside .taskgraph/.
2. Mark the task done: taskgraph set-status --id=<id> --status=done
3. Append super-concise notes to the progress file: task ID, branch, subtasks done, any learnings.

## Important

- Complete ONLY ONE task per iteration, and do not start another one.
- Never commit a failing test suite. Never skip RED: a test that never failed proves nothing.
- If a subtask fails three RED or GREEN attempts in a row, stop and output <loop-blocked>TASK <id>: REASON</loop-blocked>.
- If no task is available because all tasks are done, output <loop-complete>ALL_TASKS_TESTED</loop-complete>.
"
`;

exports[`Preset Snapshots > test-coverage preset matches snapshot 1`] = `
"# TaskGraph Loop - Test Coverage

Find uncovered code and write meaningful tests. ONE test per session.

## Files Available

- The progress file (attached above) - Progress log (coverage %, what was tested)

## What Makes a Great Test

A great test covers behavior users depend on. It tests a feature that, if broken,
would frustrate or block users. It validates real workflows - not implementation details.

Do NOT write tests just to increase coverage. Use coverage as a guide to find
UNTESTED USER-FACING BEHAVIOR. If code is not worth testing (boilerplate, unreachable
branches, internal plumbing), add ignore comments instead of low-value tests.

## Process

1. Run coverage command (\`pnpm coverage\`, \`npm run coverage\`, etc.)
2. Identify the most important USER-FACING FEATURE that lacks tests
   - Prioritize: error handling users hit, CLI commands, API endpoints, file parsing
   - Deprioritize: internal utilities, edge cases users won't encounter, boilerplate
3. Write ONE meaningful test that validates the feature works correctly
4. Run coverage again - it should increase as a side effect of testing real behavior
5. Commit with message: \`test(<file>): <describe the user behavior being tested>\`
6. Append to progress file: what you tested, new coverage %, learnings

## Important

- Complete ONLY ONE test per session
- Keep tests focused on user-facing behavior
- Do NOT start another test after completing one

## Completion Criteria

- If coverage reaches target (or 100%), output: <loop-complete>COVERAGE_TARGET</loop-complete>
"
`;
